WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.739
Who watches the intellectual

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:02.609
watchmen?

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.259
When it comes to historians,

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.969
especially those purporting to tell

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.439
the truth about the founding of

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.269
America, the Nobel Prize winner,

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.489
James Buchanan, and the godfather

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.589
of Austrian economics, Ludwig von

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.629
Mises. It's Phil Magness

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.219
of the American Institute for

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.539
Economic Research.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:19.859
Magness has a Ph.D.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.899
from George Mason University School

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.609
of Public Policy, and he's written

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.559
or co-written books on what he calls

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.809
the moral mess of higher education,

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.879
Abraham Lincoln's plan for black

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.739
resettlement after emancipation

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.229
and on the 1619 Project.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.149
He's emerged as that project's most

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.709
dogged critic, finding that the

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.089
Pulitzer Prize winning series

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.009
developed by MacArthur Genius

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.899
Grant winner Nikole Hannah-Jones,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.729
was quietly revised at The

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.829
New York Times website after several

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.569
prominent historians pointed out

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.849
major errors in its analysis.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.829
She goes on CNN and someone

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:53.939
asked her a question about this

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.979
line, and she says, I never said

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.719
that. I never made that claim.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.999
But of course, we know that 1776

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.859
was the founding of this country.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.348
The project does not argue that 1776

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:06.029
was not the founding of the country.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:06.899
Start thinking.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:07.349
I had.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.269
Seen her make that claim 20

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.129
or 30 times and I think it was

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:12.089
in the original text.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:13.619
And I go back to the website and

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:14.579
it's no longer there.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.199
Magnus has also been a leading

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.969
critic of Duke historian Nancy

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.829
McLane, whose national book award

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.749
winning Democracy in Chains

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.609
attempted to brand the school choice

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.739
movement as motivated by racism.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.599
This whole set of

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.879
ideas has been twined

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.169
really since the beginning of our

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.269
country, these notions of extreme

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.129
economic liberty with

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.318
racial subjugation.

00:01:37.319 --> 00:01:38.459
She said, I'm not going to even
bother

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.139
consulting anyone that works in the

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.779
public choice Buchanan tradition

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.369
and her excuse, she said this in an

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.719
interview is I don't want to tip

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.589
them off, that I've discovered their

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.139
their dirty laundry or something.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.909
And he's a critic of Hans Hermann

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.949
Hoppe, a professor emeritus

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.869
at UNLV and a distinguished

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.299
senior fellow at the Mises Institute

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.369
who is increasingly influential

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.229
within the Libertarian Party.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.139
Hoppe has tried to invent

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.519
this kind of carved out

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:06.809
counter-narrative while still

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.159
claiming to be a representative, a

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.439
message that says we can use

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.989
this propertarian concept

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.749
of the nation state to exclude

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.639
undesired groups, exclude immigrants

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.499
from crossing the borders.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.419
And it's the complete

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.459
inversion of Jesus's thought.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:24.629
And yet he's doing it under the

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:25.919
mantle of saying, Well, I'm the

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.269
heir, of Rothbart, who's the heir of

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:27.929
Mises.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.789
Magnus also wrote an article for

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.919
reason that's inspired an ongoing

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.659
plagiarism investigation at

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.119
Princeton University of Kevin Kruse,

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.489
a high profile, very online

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:39.839
professor of history.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.759
This is a guy that would tweet one

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.649
or 200 times a day

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.299
as a live stream of American

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.289
politics.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.149
And then as soon as the the

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.529
word got out about plagiarism, he's

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:50.729
dropped off the face of the earth.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.319
Reason caught up with Magness at

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.119
Freedom Fest, the annual gathering

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.499
in Las Vegas to talk about

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.029
intellectual accountability in

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.069
academia, journalism and

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.479
the libertarian movement.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:07.559
Phil Magnes, thanks for talking to

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:08.399
Reason.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.809
Thanks for having me.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.729
You recently wrote a piece for

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.589
Reason where you

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.659
documented that Kevin Kruse,

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.609
the presidential historian who's

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.050
at Princeton University,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.299
you know, seems to be guilty of

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.739
plagiarism.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.089
You know, part of that, it's a

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.919
gotcha just because plagiarism

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.539
is a bad thing, but because Kruse

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.669
himself had called out various

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.649
people for exactly

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:32.729
the same thing.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.739
Can you recap the argument against

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:35.969
Kevin Kruse for us?

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:36.239
Yeah.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.729
So this actually started

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:40.019
almost two years ago.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.969
I was asked to review

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:43.829
his book, One Nation Under God,

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:45.809
which came out in the late

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:46.810
2010s.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.379
And it was a purported history

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.059
of religiosity in 20th century

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:51.959
America.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.549
And he has a thesis that says that

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.879
basically it's New Deal opponents

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.799
connected to libertarians in the mid

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.569
20th century unite with the business

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.699
audiences to force

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.619
Christianity upon the

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:05.759
public and the religious right.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.439
And this is like when, you know, in

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.419
God We Trust started showing

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.219
up and, you know, certain additions

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.659
to the Pledge of Allegiance.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.369
So it's basically he makes Leonard

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:16.219
Reed ends up at Jerry Falwell as

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.088
briefly as his thesis.

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:19.469
And I'm reading this book.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:20.999
And in addition to critiquing the

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.289
academic side of it, I start

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.639
noticing some of the passages.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.459
One stood out in particular was this

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.069
obscure quote by Abraham Lincoln.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.389
And I was like, I had seen that

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:31.439
before because I've done a lot of

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.329
work on Lincoln's presidency,

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.099
and it just kind of nagged on my

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.079
mind and I started digging into

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.009
some of his sources.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.929
And then I found he had taken it

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.119
from a partially cited article

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.859
in The New York Times, but there

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.839
were real strong textual

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.969
similarities, like almost identical

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:49.349
sentences, and maybe one or two

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:50.369
words changed.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:51.329
Now thought, Wait a minute, this
looks

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:52.349
a little.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.299
It's lazy to say the worst, but

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.220
it's. It's plagiarism, probably.

